# Own-wording spells of Deep Magic (2014 rules), chunk L (Tracer to Yellow Sign). TEXT = {name: text}.
TEXT = {
"Tracer": "When you cast this spell, and as a bonus action on each of your turns until it ends, you can place a tiny invisible beacon on a piece of ammunition you shoot from a ranged weapon. If a ranged attack roll with that ammunition hits a target, the beacon moves onto the target. Your weapon is tuned to the beacon and grows warm whenever it points toward the target, as long as the target is on your plane of existence. You can have only one tracer target at a time. Putting a tracer on a new target ends the effect on the old one. A creature can notice the magical beacon with an Intelligence (Arcana) check against your spell save DC.",
"Tracking Beacon": "You pick a creature within range and mark it with a glowing orb of light that floats 120 feet above its head. The target makes a Wisdom saving throw. On a success, the spell ends. On a failure, the orb stays over the target and follows it as it moves, so you can tell its direction and distance as long as the beacon is in your sight. The beacon keeps working when you can't see it, as long as the target stays on your plane. If you or the target leave the plane, the spell ends. As a bonus action on your turn, you can lower the beacon from its starting height of 120 feet to as low as 5 feet above the target's head.",
"Transmogrification": "You raise your body to a higher form. When you cast the spell, choose three of the forms below and decide which one takes effect first. While the spell lasts, you can use a bonus action to swap one of your choices for an unused form, or to pick a new form when the current one ends. The spell ends when its duration runs out or when you have used up the third form you took.\n\n**Wings.** You grow batlike or feathered wings and gain a flying speed of 60 feet.\n\n**Breath Weapon.** Choose acid, cold, fire, lightning, or poison. As an action, you breathe a line 15 feet long and 5 feet wide. Each creature in it takes 8d6 damage of that type, or half as much on a successful Dexterity save. This is a magical attack. You can do this up to three times, then the form ends.\n\n**Limbs.** Tentacles, spider legs, pincers, or similar limbs grow from your body. Choose slashing, bludgeoning, or piercing damage. As an action, you make two melee weapon attacks with the limbs, each dealing 2d12 damage of the chosen type. You are proficient with them, and they have reach and the finesse property.\n\n**Radiating Light.** You shed light in a 10-foot radius, from a halo, ghostly flames, your eyes, or another source you choose. The light deals necrotic, fire, or radiant damage (your choice). A creature that passes through the light on its turn or ends its turn in it takes 2d10 damage of that type, or half as much on a successful Constitution save. A creature takes this damage only once per turn.\n\n**Regeneration.** You regain 10 hit points at the start of your turn. You can do this up to three times, then the form ends.\n\n**Overwhelming Might.** When you hit with a weapon attack, the target takes an extra 2d8 force damage and must succeed on a Strength save or be knocked prone.\n\n**Frightening Presence.** As an action, choose any number of creatures within 30 feet that can see you. Each must succeed on a Wisdom save or be frightened of you for 1 minute. A frightened creature repeats the save at the end of each of its turns, ending the effect on a success. A creature that succeeds on the save, or whose effect ends, is immune to this for 24 hours. You can do this up to three times, then the form ends.",
"Treasure Chasm": "You haze the sight of one creature within range with the glint of a gold coin. The target makes a Wisdom saving throw. On a failure, it sees a gorge, trench, or hole in the ground at a spot you choose within range, brimming with gold and treasure. On its next turn it must move toward that spot. When it arrives, it is incapacitated while it scoops up imaginary treasure. An affected creature repeats the save at the end of each of its turns, ending the effect on itself on a success. The effect also ends if you or your allies damage the creature. A creature of the dragon type has disadvantage on the first save but advantage on later saves made after it reaches the spot.",
"Tree Heal": "You touch a plant and it regains 1d4 hit points. Instead, you can cure it of one disease or clear it of pests. After you cast this spell on a plant or plant creature, you can't cast it on the same target again for 24 hours. It works only on plants and plant creatures.",
"Tree Running": "One willing creature you touch gains a climbing speed equal to its walking speed. The climbing speed works only while the creature is touching a living plant or fungus growing from the ground. It can cling to a suitable surface with one hand or just its feet, which leaves its hands free for weapons or spells. The plant doesn't bend under its weight, so the creature can walk along the thinnest twig, stand on a leaf, or run over the tops of wheat stalks without bending one or touching the ground.",
"Tree Speak": "You touch a tree and ask it one question about anything that might have happened close to it, such as who passed by. You get a mental impression of the answer, which lasts for the duration. Trees don't track time like humanoids, so the tree might tell of last night or of a hundred years ago. The impression can include sight, sound, vibration, or smell, all from the tree's point of view. Trees watch closely for anything that threatens the forest and always report it when asked. If the tree holds a creature that can merge with trees, such as a dryad, you can freely talk with that creature for the duration.",
"Trench": "With scooping gestures, you make the ground slowly sink along a line 5 feet wide and 60 feet long that starts at a point within range. When you finish casting, a trench 5 feet deep remains. The spell works only on flat, open ground (not stone or paved surfaces) that is free of creatures and objects.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** When you cast this spell using a spell slot of 3rd level or higher, you can increase the width of the trench by 5 feet or the length by 30 feet for each slot level above 2nd. You can choose width or length separately for each slot level above 2nd.",
"Trick Question": "You ask a question that can be answered with one word, aimed at a creature within range that can hear you. The target makes a Wisdom saving throw. On a failure, it is compelled to answer truthfully. Once it answers, it knows that you used magic to compel it.",
"Triumph of Ice": "You turn one of the four elements (air, earth, fire, or water) into ice or snow across a sphere with a 100-foot radius centered on you. The effect depends on the element.\n\n**Air.** Vapor condenses into falling snow. This negates a fog cloud, stinking cloud, or similar magic in the area. A creature of elemental air in range takes 8d6 cold damage. If it is airborne, it must also succeed on a Constitution save at the start of its turn or be knocked prone, with no falling damage.\n\n**Earth.** The soil freezes into permafrost to a depth of 10 feet. A creature burrowing through the area has its speed halved until it thaws, unless it can burrow through solid rock. A creature of elemental earth in range must succeed on a Constitution save or take 8d6 cold damage.\n\n**Fire.** Flames and other extreme heat on the ground, such as molten lava, turn to shards of ice, and the area they fill becomes difficult terrain. Each creature in the formerly burning area takes 2d6 slashing damage when you cast the spell, and 1d6 slashing damage for every 5 feet it moves in the area until the spell ends (unless icy terrain doesn't hinder it). A successful Dexterity save halves this damage. A creature of elemental fire in range must succeed on a Constitution save or take 8d6 cold damage and be stunned for 1d6 rounds.\n\n**Water.** Open water such as a pond, lake, or river freezes to a depth of 4 feet. A creature on the surface when it freezes must succeed on a Dexterity save or be trapped in the ice. A trapped creature can use an action to free itself with a successful Strength (Athletics) check. A creature of elemental water in range takes no damage, but it is paralyzed for 1d6 rounds unless it succeeds on a Constitution save, and it treats the area as difficult terrain.",
"True Light of Revelation": "A golden radiance spreads from you, giving bright light out to 30 feet and dim light for another 30 feet. Invisible creatures and objects in the bright light become visible while within 30 feet of you. Magical disguises and illusions within 30 feet of you turn faint and transparent, so their false nature is obvious. These effects return to full strength if the disguise or illusion moves more than 30 feet from you, or you move that far from it. A creature that has taken another form, through a spell such as polymorph or shapechange or through an ability such as a druid's wild shape, must succeed on a Constitution save when it enters the bright light or be forced back into its true form. A creature in the bright light that tries to change shape must also succeed on a Constitution save to do so.",
"Twist the Skein": "You nudge a thread of fate as a creature makes an attack roll, saving throw, or skill check. Roll a d20 and subtract 10, which gives a number from 10 down to -9. Add that number to the creature's roll. This can turn a failure into a success or the reverse, or change nothing. The creature must use the modified result, whether it is better or worse than the original.",
"Umbral Storm": "You open a channel to a region of the Plane of Shadow that is hostile to life and order. A storm of dark, raging entropy fills a sphere with a 20-foot radius centered on a point you can see within range. A creature that starts its turn in the storm, or enters it for the first time on its turn, takes 6d8 necrotic damage and gains one level of exhaustion. A successful Constitution save halves the damage and prevents the exhaustion. As a bonus action on your turn, you can move the storm up to 30 feet in any direction.",
"Uncanny Avoidance": "You extend a hand and point at a creature within range. Your magic sharpens its awareness of attacks aimed at it. The next attack roll made against the creature before the spell ends has disadvantage.",
"Uncontrollable Transformation": "You fill your body with raw chaos and will it to take on a helpful mutation. Roll a d10 and read the result on the table below. You can try to steer the change toward a mutation you prefer, but it is taxing. You can roll the d10 twice and pick the result you like, but you gain one level of exhaustion. When the spell ends, your body returns to its normal form.\n\n**1.** A thin third arm grows from your shoulder. As a bonus action, you can attack with a light weapon using it. You have advantage on Dexterity (Sleight of Hand) checks and on checks that involve handling tools.\n\n**2.** Rough scales cover your skin. Your AC increases by 1 and you gain resistance to a random damage type (roll on the Random Damage Type table in the frenzied bolt spell).\n\n**3.** A puckered opening grows on your back. You can blast air from it to gain a flying speed of 30 feet, and you must land at the end of your turn. As a bonus action, you can push a creature with the blast. It is pushed 5 feet away from you if it fails a Strength save with a DC of 10 + your Constitution modifier.\n\n**4.** A second face appears on the back of your head. You gain darkvision out to 120 feet and advantage on Wisdom (Perception) checks that rely on sight or smell. You become good at holding conversations with yourself.\n\n**5.** You grow gills that let you breathe underwater and filter poison from the air. You are immune to inhaled poisons.\n\n**6.** Your hindquarters lengthen and you grow a second set of legs. Your walking speed increases by 10 feet and your carrying capacity becomes your Strength score times 20.\n\n**7.** You become incorporeal and can move through creatures and objects as if they were difficult terrain. You take 1d10 force damage if you end your turn inside an object. You can't pick up or use physical objects that you weren't already carrying when you became incorporeal.\n\n**8.** Your limbs lengthen and flatten into prehensile paddles. You gain a swimming speed equal to your walking speed and advantage on Strength (Athletics) checks to climb or swim. Your unarmed strikes deal 1d6 bludgeoning damage.\n\n**9.** Your head fills with light gas and swells to four times its size, and your hair falls out. You have advantage on Intelligence and Wisdom ability checks and can levitate up to 5 feet above the ground.\n\n**10.** You grow three pairs of feathered wings. You gain a flying speed equal to your walking speed and can hover.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** When you cast this spell using a spell slot of 8th level or higher, you gain one more mutation for each slot level above 7th. You gain one level of exhaustion for each mutation whose outcome you try to control.",
"Undermine Armor": "You unravel the forces that hold a suit of armor together. A target wearing armor must succeed on a Constitution save or its armor softens to the texture of candle wax, which lowers the target's AC by 2. The spell has no effect on a creature that isn't wearing armor.",
"Unholy Defiance": "Until the spell ends, undead creatures within range have advantage on saving throws against effects that turn undead. If an undead creature in the area has the Turning Defiance trait, it can roll a d4 and add the result to a save against an effect that turns undead.",
"Unleash Effigy": "You animate a stone statue that you can see within 60 feet of you, and it becomes your ally. It uses the statistics of a stone golem and takes its turn right after yours. As a bonus action on your turn, you can order it to move and attack, as long as you are within 60 feet of it. Without orders, the statue does nothing. At the start of your turn, if the statue has 75 hit points or fewer, or is more than 60 feet from you, you must succeed on a DC 16 spellcasting check or the statue goes berserk. A berserk statue attacks you or one of your allies on each of its turns. If no creature is close enough to attack, it dashes toward the nearest one. Once berserk, it stays berserk until destroyed. When the spell ends, the statue becomes an ordinary statue again.",
"Unluck on That": "With a quick curse, you bring misfortune to a creature's attempt. The affected creature has disadvantage on the roll.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** When you cast this spell using a spell slot of 2nd level or higher, the range increases by 5 feet for each slot level above 1st.",
"Unruly Item": "You animate an object held by a creature within range, giving it limited movement and a drive to thwart its owner. The effect depends on the object.\n\n**Weapon.** A wielded weapon twists and struggles to break free. The creature makes a Dexterity save. On a failure, it has disadvantage on attacks with that weapon this round. If it fails by 5 or more, it drops the weapon.\n\n**Armor.** The creature's armor shifts and clanks and becomes uncomfortable. If the armor doesn't normally impose disadvantage on Dexterity (Stealth) checks, it does while this spell lasts. The wearer must succeed on a Dexterity save or take a 10-foot penalty to its speed and be unable to take the Dash action.\n\n**Other Items.** The GM decides how other carried items behave. A potion might fight being drunk, and if the save fails by 5 or more it falls and is ruined. A backpack might try to empty itself and spill a random item each round on a failed save.",
"Unseen Strangler": "You conjure an immaterial, tentacled aberration in an unoccupied space you can see within range, and you set a password that it recognizes. It stays where you conjured it until the spell ends, until you dismiss it as an action, or until you move more than 80 feet from it. The strangler is invisible to everyone but you and can't be harmed. When a Small or larger creature comes within 30 feet of it without speaking the password, the strangler starts whispering your name. You always hear the whisper while you are conscious, whatever other noise there is. The strangler sees invisible creatures and into the Ethereal Plane, and it ignores illusions.\n\nIf any creature hostile to you is within 5 feet of the strangler at the start of your turn, the strangler attacks one of them with a tentacle. It makes a melee weapon attack with a bonus equal to your spellcasting ability modifier + your proficiency bonus. On a hit, it deals 3d6 bludgeoning damage, and a Large or smaller target is grappled (escape DC = your spellcasting ability modifier + your proficiency bonus). While the grapple lasts, the target is restrained and the strangler can't attack anyone else. On a critical hit, the target begins to suffocate and can't speak until the grapple ends.",
"Unshackled Magic": "You choose a creature within range that has the Spellcasting or Innate Spellcasting trait and fill it with wild magic, so it loses control of its magical ability. The target makes a Charisma save. On a failure, on its next turn it picks one of its available spells at random and casts it as an action. If the spell can be cast with a higher slot, roll randomly to pick the slot. The target still decides things like where the area goes and who is affected, but it must cast that spell at that time. Each round while the spell lasts, the target makes another Charisma save. On a success, it acts normally that round, but the spell does not end.",
"Vine Trestle": "This spell can be cast only as a ritual. You make a vine sprout from the ground and creep along a surface or rise into the air in a direction you choose. It must grow from a solid surface such as the ground or a wall, and it can bear 600 pounds along its whole length. If that limit is exceeded, the vine collapses at once. A vine that collapses from weight or damage disintegrates immediately. Its many small shoots make it climbable with a successful DC 5 Strength (Athletics) check. It has AC 8, hit points equal to 5 times your spellcasting level, and a damage threshold of 5.\n\n**Group Spellcasting.** When other spellcasters help you cast this spell, the vine can bear an extra 30 pounds and its damage threshold rises by 1 for each caster other than you, up to a maximum of 810 pounds and a damage threshold of 12.\n\n**Ritual Focus.** If you expend your ritual focus, the vine is permanent until it is destroyed or dispelled.",
"Visage of Madness": "Your face briefly becomes that of a demon lord, horrifying enough to drive foes mad. Every enemy within 30 feet of you that can see you makes a Wisdom saving throw. On a failure, a creature claws at its own eyes and deals piercing damage to itself equal to 1d6 + its Strength modifier. It is also stunned until the end of its next turn and blinded for 1d4 rounds. A creature that rolls the maximum on the d6 (a 6) is blinded permanently.",
"Visage of the Dead": "You fill yourself and up to four creatures you touch with necromantic energy, so you take on aspects of the undead. For the duration, the targets are immune to poison damage and the poisoned condition, and they look like undead versions of themselves. An undead creature that meets an affected creature must make an Intelligence save against your spell save DC. On a failure, it mistakes the target for another undead. Usually this means it ignores the target, especially if it is mindless like a skeleton or zombie, unless it is ordered otherwise. A smarter undead may react depending on the circumstances, but if the target's presence isn't unusual, it will probably ignore the target unless the target acts suspiciously.",
"Vital Mark": "You mark an unattended magic item, including weapons and armor, with a clearly visible stain of your blood. You decide how the stain looks. The item's magical abilities don't work for anyone else while the stain remains. For example, a +1 flaming longsword with a vital mark works as a plain nonmagical longsword for everyone but you, but it still works as a +1 flaming longsword for you, the caster who stained it. A wand of magic missiles would be just a stick in anyone else's hands.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** When you cast this spell using a spell slot of 4th level or higher on the same item for 28 consecutive days, the effect becomes permanent until dispelled.",
"Void Rift": "You speak a hideous string of Void Speech that leaves your mouth bloodied, and a rift into nothingness tears open. The rift is a sphere with a 10-foot radius that forms around a point you can see within range. The area within 40 feet of the sphere's outer edge becomes difficult terrain as the Void tries to draw everything in. A creature that starts its turn within 40 feet of the sphere, or enters that area for the first time on its turn, must succeed on a Strength save or be pulled 15 feet toward the rift. A creature that starts its turn in the rift, or touches it for the first time on its turn, takes 8d10 necrotic damage. Creatures inside the rift are blinded and deafened. Unattended objects within 40 feet of the rift are pulled 15 feet toward it at the start of your turn, and they take damage as creatures do if they touch it. While you concentrate on the spell, you take 2d6 necrotic damage at the end of each of your turns.",
"Void Strike": "With a short phrase of Void Speech, you gather writhing darkness around your hand. When you cast the spell, and as an action on later turns while you keep concentrating, you can fire a bolt of darkness at a creature within range. Make a ranged spell attack. If the target is in dim light or darkness, you have advantage on the roll. On a hit, the target takes 5d8 necrotic damage and is frightened of you until the start of your next turn.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** When you cast this spell using a spell slot of 4th level or higher, the damage increases by 1d8 for each slot level above 3rd.",
"Volley Shield": "You touch a willing creature and surround it with a shimmering shield of energy. The shield gives the target a +5 bonus to AC and resistance to nonmagical bludgeoning, piercing, and slashing damage for the duration. The shield can also throw hostile spells back. When the target succeeds on a saving throw against a hostile spell, the caster of that spell becomes its new target. The caster gets the appropriate saving throw, if any, and is affected as if the spell had come from a caster of the caster's own level.",
"Vomit Tentacles": "Your jaws stretch wide and dozens of thin, slimy tentacles pour out of your mouth to seize your enemies. Make a melee spell attack against a foe within 15 feet of you. On a hit, the target takes bludgeoning damage equal to 2d6 + your Strength modifier and is grappled (escape DC equal to your spell save DC). While the grapple lasts, the target is restrained and takes the same damage at the start of each of your turns. You can grapple only one creature at a time. The tentacles have an AC equal to yours. If they take slashing damage equal to 5 + your Constitution modifier from a single attack, enough of them are cut to let a grappled creature go. Severed tentacles are replaced at the start of your turn. Damage to the tentacles doesn't reduce your hit points. While the spell is active, you can't speak and can't cast spells with verbal components.",
"Voorish Sign": "For the duration, invisible creatures and objects within 20 feet of you become visible to you, and you have advantage on saving throws against effects that cause the frightened condition. The effect moves with you and stays centered on you until the duration ends.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** When you cast this spell using a spell slot of 3rd level or higher, the radius increases by 5 feet for every two slot levels above 1st.",
"Waft": "Dragon parents invented this spell to help their young learn to fly. You gain a flying speed of 60 feet for 1 round. At the start of your next turn, you drift down and land gently on a solid surface below you.",
"Walk the Twisted Path": "You and up to five creatures you can see within 20 feet of you enter a shifting landscape of endless walls and corridors that link to many places in the world. You can find your way to a destination within 100 miles, as long as you are certain it exists (you don't need to have seen or visited it), and you must succeed on a DC 20 Intelligence check. If you can retrace a path you've taken before without a check, as a minotaur or goristro can, the check succeeds automatically. On a failed check, you don't find your path this round, and you and your companions each take 4d6 psychic damage as the madness of the maze takes its toll. You repeat the check at the start of each of your turns until you find your way or die. Either way, the spell ends. When it ends, you and those traveling with you appear in a safe location at your destination.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** When you cast this spell using a spell slot of 7th level or higher, you can bring two more creatures or travel 100 more miles for each slot level above 6th.",
"Walking Wall": "You make a wall of swinging axes from a pile of miniature axes that you provide as you cast. The wall fills a rectangle 10 feet wide, 10 feet high, and 20 feet long. It has a base speed of 50 feet but can't take the Dash action. On your turn it can make up to four attacks, using your spell attack modifier to hit, with a reach of 10 feet. You direct its movement and attacks as a bonus action. If you don't, it keeps carrying out your last command. The wall can't use reactions. Each hit deals 4d6 slashing damage, which counts as magical. The wall has AC 12 and 200 hit points, and it is immune to necrotic, poison, psychic, and piercing damage. If it drops to 0 hit points, or when the spell ends, the wall disappears and the miniature axes fall to the ground in a neat heap.",
"Wall of Time": "You make a wall of shimmering, transparent blocks on a solid surface within range. It can be a straight wall up to 60 feet long, 20 feet high, and 1 foot thick, or a cylinder up to 20 feet high, 1 foot thick, and 20 feet across. Nonmagical ranged attacks that cross the wall vanish into the time stream with no other effect. Ranged spell attacks, and ranged weapon attacks made with magic weapons, that pass through the wall are made with disadvantage. A creature that deliberately enters or passes through the wall is affected as if it had just failed its first save against a slow spell.",
"Warning Shout": "You sense danger before it arrives and call a warning to an ally. One creature that you can see and that can hear you gains advantage on its initiative roll.",
"Warp Mind and Matter": "You can cast this spell only as a ritual. A creature you can see within range undergoes a baleful transformation. The target must succeed on a Wisdom save or suffer a flesh warp and be afflicted with a form of indefinite madness. Greater restoration or comparable magic ends the madness, and regenerate or comparable magic removes the flesh warp.",
"Wave of Corruption": "Necromantic energy spreads in a 30-foot radius around you, corrupting what it touches. Each unattended, nonmagical object in range takes 3d8 necrotic damage. All food and drink in the area that isn't being carried is fouled and unpalatable. Magic consumables such as potions have a 50 percent chance of being ruined, but one held by a creature lets its owner make a Constitution saving throw, using the owner's save bonus, to avoid that outcome.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** When you cast this spell using a spell slot of 5th level or higher, the necrotic damage increases by 1d8 for every two slot levels above 3rd.",
"Wayward Strike": "You tear a hole in reality to shield yourself from an incoming attack, sending it through a second tear that opens next to a creature you can see within range. The attack goes in one tear and out the other, and is resolved against that creature's AC instead. If the attack succeeds, the creature takes the damage as normal. You can even make the attacker hit itself.",
"Weapon of Blood": "You cause yourself 1d4 slashing damage that can't be healed until the blade this spell creates is destroyed or the spell ends. The trickling blood turns into a dagger of red metal that works as a +1 dagger.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Cast with a spell slot of 3rd to 5th level, the wound deals 3d4 slashing damage and the spell produces a +2 dagger. Cast with a slot of 6th to 8th level, the wound deals 6d4 slashing damage and the spell produces a +2 dagger of wounding. Cast with a 9th-level slot, the wound deals 9d4 slashing damage and the spell produces a +3 dagger of wounding.",
"Weiler's Ward": "You create four small orbs of faerie magic that float around your head and shed dim light in a 15-foot radius. When a Large or smaller enemy enters that dim light, or starts its turn in it, you can use your reaction to attack it with one or more of the orbs. The enemy makes a Charisma save. On a failure, it is pushed 20 feet directly away from you, and each orb you used in the attack bursts and deals 1d6 force damage to it.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** When you cast this spell using a spell slot of 3rd level or higher, the number of orbs increases by one for each slot level above 2nd.",
"Wild Shield": "You wrap yourself in the forces of chaos. Wild lights and odd sounds surround you, which makes stealth impossible. While the spell is active, you can use a reaction to repel a spell of 4th level or lower that targets you or whose area you are in. A repelled spell has no effect on you, but doing so risks a chaos magic surge as if you had cast a spell, with you counted as the caster for any effect of the surge. The spell ends when its duration runs out or when it has absorbed 4 levels of spells. If you try to repel a spell whose level is higher than the levels you have left, make an ability check with your spellcasting ability. The DC equals 10 + the spell's level - the number of levels the shield can still repel. On a success, the spell is repelled. On a failure, it takes full effect. The chance of a surge exists whether or not the spell is repelled.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** When you cast this spell using a spell slot of 5th level or higher, you can repel one additional spell level for each slot level above 4th.",
"Wild Trajectory": "You create a cube of magical instability, 30 feet on a side, that sends spell effects passing through it off their original paths. When a spell with a visible or physical manifestation passes through the area, such as fireball, lightning bolt, or a ray or missile like magic missile or ray of frost, roll a d8. On a 1, the spell continues on its original path or toward its original target. On a 2, it veers 45 degrees to the right. On a 3, 90 degrees to the right. On a 4, 135 degrees to the right. On a 5, it turns 180 degrees and comes back the way it came, striking the caster if it is a targeted spell. On a 6, it veers 45 degrees to the left. On a 7, 90 degrees to the left. On an 8, 135 degrees to the left. A spell that affects everything along its path, such as lightning bolt, keeps doing so along the new path up to its range limit. A spell such as fireball travels in the new direction to the limit of its range before it detonates. A targeted spell strikes the first valid target it meets on the new path.",
"Wind Lash": "A quick gesture makes a solid lash of howling wind. Make a melee spell attack against the target. On a hit, the target takes 1d8 slashing damage from the shearing wind and is pushed 5 feet away from you. The damage increases by 1d8 when you reach 5th level (2d8), 11th level (3d8), and 17th level (4d8).",
"Wind Tunnel": "You create a swirling tunnel of strong wind that runs from you in a direction you choose. The tunnel is a line 60 feet long and 10 feet wide, and once made it stays in place. The wind blows from you toward the far end. A creature in the line moving with the wind (away from you) adds 10 feet to its speed, and ranged weapon attacks made with the wind don't have disadvantage from long range. A creature moving against the wind (toward you) spends 2 feet of movement for each foot it moves, and ranged weapon attacks made along the line against the wind have disadvantage. The tunnel instantly scatters gas and vapor and puts out candles, torches, and similar unprotected flames in the line. Protected flames, such as a lantern's, dance wildly and have a 50 percent chance of going out.",
"Wind of the Hereafter": "You create a roiling sphere of wind with a 30-foot radius that carries the choking stench of death, centered on a point you choose within range. The wind blows around corners. When a creature starts its turn in the sphere, it takes 8d8 necrotic damage, or half as much on a successful Constitution save. Creatures are affected even if they hold their breath or don't need to breathe. At the start of each of your turns, the sphere moves 10 feet away from you, drifting along the ground. It isn't heavier than air, but it drifts in a straight line for the duration, even over a cliff or gully. If it meets a wall or other impassable obstacle, it turns left or right (chosen at random).",
"Winding Key": "You target a construct and give it an extra action or move on each of its turns.",
"Winter's Radiance": "The piercing glare of a full day's sunlight on fresh snow floods a 30-foot cube centered on a point within range. A creature in the area must succeed on a Constitution save or have disadvantage on ranged attacks and Wisdom (Perception) checks for the duration. An affected creature's vision is also so hampered that foes it targets count as having three-quarters cover.",
"Winterdark": "This spell calls up the deepest part of the night on the winter solstice. You target a cylinder with a 40-foot radius and 60-foot height, centered on a point within range, and plunge it into darkness and unbearable cold. Each creature in the area when you cast the spell, and at the start of each of its turns, must succeed on a Constitution save or take 1d6 cold damage and gain one level of exhaustion. Creatures immune to cold damage are also immune to the exhaustion, as are creatures wearing cold weather gear or otherwise adapted to a cold environment. As a bonus action, you can move the center of the effect 20 feet.",
"Wintry Glide": "You can travel through ice or snow without crossing the space in between. If you are next to a mass of ice or snow, you can enter it by spending 5 feet of movement. By spending another 5 feet of movement, you can leave it at once at any point within 500 feet that belongs to the same connected mass of ice or snow. When you enter, you instantly know how far the material extends within 500 feet. You must have at least 10 feet of movement available when you cast the spell or it fails. If the mass of ice or snow is destroyed while you are passing through it, you must succeed on a Constitution save against your spell save DC or take 4d6 bludgeoning damage and fall prone at the midpoint of the line between where you entered and where you meant to exit.",
"Withered Sight": "You make the eyes of a creature you can see within range lose their sharpness. The target makes a Constitution save. On a failure, it has disadvantage on Wisdom (Perception) checks and on all attack rolls for the duration. An affected creature repeats the save at the end of each of its turns, ending the effect on itself on a success. The spell has no effect on a creature that is blind or doesn't use eyes to see.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** When you cast this spell using a spell slot of 2nd level or higher, you can target one additional creature for each slot level above 1st.",
"Withering Smite": "Your weapon gives off terrible energy that empowers your next strike. The next time you hit with a melee weapon attack during the spell, the attack deals an extra 1d6 necrotic damage. If the target is a creature, it must also succeed on a Constitution save or take 1d6 necrotic damage at the start of each of its turns for 1 minute as its flesh rots around the wound. An affected creature repeats the save at the end of each of its turns, ending the effect on a success. The rot also ends if the creature receives magical healing.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** When you cast this spell using a spell slot of 2nd level or higher, the extra damage from the hit increases by 1d6 for each slot level above 1st. The later necrotic damage doesn't increase.",
"Wolfsong": "You let out a howl that carries 300 feet outdoors. The howl conveys a message of up to nine words that all dogs and wolves in the area understand, along with one specific creature of any kind that you name when you cast the spell, if you choose. Cast indoors above ground, the howl carries 200 feet. Cast underground, it carries 100 feet. A creature that understands the message isn't forced to act in any way, though the message may suggest or even dictate a course of action.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** When you cast this spell using a spell slot of 2nd level or higher, you can name one more specific recipient for each slot level above 2nd.",
"Word of Misfortune": "You hiss a word of Void Speech and choose one creature you can see within range. The next time the target makes a saving throw during the spell's duration, it must roll a d4 and subtract the result from the total of the save. The spell then ends.",
"Wresting Wind": "You blow a pinch of confetti from your cupped hand and make a burst of air that tears weapons and other items from your enemies' hands. Each enemy in a 20-foot radius centered on a point you choose within range must succeed on a Strength save or drop whatever it is holding. The objects land 10 feet from the creatures that dropped them, in random directions.",
"Write Memory": "You copy your own memories, or memories you learned with the read memory spell, onto an empty memory gear.",
"Writhing Arms": "Your arms turn into constantly writhing tentacles. As an action, you can make a melee spell attack against a target within range. The target takes 1d10 necrotic damage and is grappled (escape DC equal to your spell save DC). If the target doesn't escape, you can use your action on each later turn to deal 1d10 necrotic damage to it automatically. The tentacles make handling items difficult. You can't wield weapons or hold objects, including material components, while the spell lasts.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** When you cast this spell using a spell slot of 2nd level or higher, the damage from your tentacle attack increases by 1d10 for each slot level above 1st.",
"Yellow Sign": "You try to afflict a humanoid you can see within range with memories of distant, alien realms and their strange inhabitants. The target must succeed on a Wisdom save or suffer a form of long-term madness and be charmed by you for 1d10 hours or until you or one of your allies harms it in any way. While charmed this way, the creature sees you as a sacred monarch. If you or an ally of yours is fighting the creature, it has advantage on its save. A successful remove curse spell ends both effects.",
}
